#WRITE(PTR, $00001E66)
I left the café and started rushing back to my base.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001E76)
In the end, my anxiety had been too much to handle, and I’d left%Nalmost immediately after I saw the girl.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001E86)
I wanted to get home as soon as possible, but the crowded sidewalks were slowing me down.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001E96)
I was feeling sicker and sicker by the minute.
<LINE>
I hated crowds... I always felt like people were watching me and%Nsnickering.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001EA6)
A red pedestrian signal stopped me in my tracks. As I waited for%Nthe light to change again, I couldn’t help but take a quick look%Naround.
<LINE>
I didn’t see Girl A anywhere.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001F52)
As soon as I reached the top of Dogenzaka, walking got a lot%Neasier.
<LINE>
Usually I would take the least-crowded way back, but...
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00001FCA)
After what had happened two nights ago, I figured I should stick to the main streets. Just to be safe.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00002068)
I was stuck at a red light again, so I took another look behind me. But Girl A was still nowhere to be found.
<LINE>
The girl I’d seen at @café might’ve been a different person after%Nall.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00002078)
Feeling a bit relieved, I let my tense shoulders relax a little,%Nthen turned back around...
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000023BE)
【拓巳】
“...!”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000023CE)
Shocked, I took a step back.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000023DE)
On the other side of the crosswalk...
<LINE>
...standing among the crowd, waiting for the light to change...%N...was the girl.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000023EE)
And to make matters worse, the gaze beyond those glasses was%Nclearly pointed straight at me. She didn’t even try to look away.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000023FE)
Her eyes were so intense... it was as if they were urging me to do%Nsomething.
<END>
